CH = dict(num=7, title="The Age of Reorganisation", tag="History", theme="Post-Mauryan kingdoms, South India, foreign rulers, art",
 intro="After the Mauryas (about 185 BCE) the empire broke into many competing kingdoms, and the north-west lay open to invaders. Yet this 'Age of Reorganisation' (2nd century BCE to 3rd century CE) was also a time of lively trade, new art styles and cultural blending. This chapter surveys the Shungas, Satavahanas, Chedis, southern kingdoms, Indo-Greeks, Shakas and Kushanas.",
 big_questions=["Why is the period after the Mauryas called the 'Age of Reorganisation'?", "What values guided rulers of that period?", "How did foreign invaders merge into Indian society and create cultural confluence?"],
 blocks=[
 ("h2", "7.1 Setting the Stage"),
 ("ul", ["The last Maurya emperor was assassinated about **185 BCE** by his commander-in-chief **Pushyamitra Shunga**. The empire broke up; former tributary kingdoms became independent, and the weakened **north-west** was exposed to invasions.",
  "Scholars call this the **Age of Reorganisation**: existing regions were being rearranged into new kingdoms that constantly competed. Kingdoms grew through **marriage (matrimonial) alliances** or war; control of border areas was vital for safety.",
  "Alongside politics there was a burst of art, architecture, literature and cultural exchange."]),
 ("table", ["Dynasties from within the Subcontinent", "Dynasties from outside"], [
  ["Shungas, Chedis, Satavahanas, Cholas, Cheras, Pandyas", "Indo-Greeks, Shakas (Indo-Scythians), Kushanas"]]),
 ("h2", "7.2 The Shungas"),
 ("ul", ["**Pushyamitra Shunga** founded the dynasty, ruling parts of north and central India. His realm was smaller than the Mauryas'; he defended it and kept friendly relations with the Greeks after early clashes. Rule ended about a century later.",
  "He performed the **ashvamedha yajna** (Vedic ritual in which a horse accompanied by soldiers roams freely; land it crosses unchallenged becomes the king's; if stopped, there is a battle). Vedic rituals revived, but other schools still thrived.",
  "**Sanskrit** became a favoured language for philosophy and literature. **Patanjali** compiled the **Yoga Sutras**.",
  "Art: the **Bharhut Stupa** (Madhya Pradesh) — carved railings and reliefs of the Buddha's life stories (some of the earliest Buddhist art), Lakshmi carvings, singers and dancers, elephants holding the wheel of dharma. Terracotta figures, ivory combs, beads and gold-covered bangles show everyday life."]),
 ("h2", "7.3 The Satavahanas"),
 ("ul", ["Ruled much of the **Deccan** (Andhra Pradesh, Telangana, Maharashtra) from the 2nd century BCE; also called **Andhras**. Capitals: **Amaravati** and **Pratishthana (Paithan)**.",
  "**Economy**: agriculture in the **Krishna-Godavari** system; vigorous trade, even with the **Roman Empire** — exports: spices, textiles, sandalwood, ivory, gold-plated pearls; imports: glass, perfumed ointments. Tolls and taxes added revenue; coins with **ships** show maritime trade and advanced shipbuilding. **Naneghat caves** near Pune served for toll collection and as traders' rest places.",
  "**Society**: princes often took their **mother's name** — **Gautamiputra Satakarni**, son of the powerful queen **Gautami Balashri**, who donated land to Buddhist monks (Nashik inscription). A **widow queen** at Naneghat performed Vedic rituals including the ashvamedha and made large donations (*dana*) of land, cows, horses, elephants and silver coins. Inscriptions in Brahmi include early **numerals** resembling modern ones — evidence of India's origin of numerals.",
  "**Religion**: devotees of Vasudeva (Krishna) but patrons of all; tax-free land to Vedic scholars and Jain and Buddhist monks. **Karla caves** (Lonavala) are for Buddhist monks; the **Pitalkhora yaksha** bears the line 'made by Kanhadasa, a goldsmith' (a goldsmith carving stone).",
  "Decline: in the **3rd century CE** weak central control and economic decline split the empire."]),
 ("h2", "7.4 The Chedis of Kalinga"),
 ("ul", ["After the Mauryas, **Kalinga** rose under the **Chedi** dynasty. **Kharavela**, a Jain devotee called *bhikshu-raja* (monk-king), respected all sects.",
  "**Udayagiri-Khandagiri caves** near Bhubaneswar: rock-cut Jain monk caves with panels and statues; the **Hathigumpha inscription** (Brahmi) records Kharavela's year-wise victories and public works, his council of ascetics and sages, and his pride in being a 'respecter of every sect and repairer of every temple' — the Indian ethos of protecting all schools of thought."]),
 ("h2", "7.5 The South: Cheras, Cholas, Pandyas"),
 ("ul", ["Between the 2nd-3rd century BCE and 3rd century CE three kingdoms dominated the south; Ashoka's empire stopped at them (they remained independent).",
  "**Sangam literature** (Sangam from *sangha* = association; an assembly of poets) is the oldest in south India: anthologies on love, heroism, generosity; key source for historians; the era is the **Sangam Age**. The Sangams mention three 'crowned kings'."]),
 ("table", ["Kingdom", "Capital / area", "Key facts"], [
  ["Cholas", "Uraiyur; Puhar (Kaveripattinam); Kaveri delta; ruled 3rd century BCE to 13th century CE", "**Karikala** defeated Chera-Pandya alliance; built the **Kallanai (Grand Anicut)** on the Kaveri below Srirangam, still in use, making the delta the 'rice bowl of the South'"],
  ["Cheras (Keralaputra)", "Vanji (Karur); west Tamil Nadu and Kerala", "Patrons of Sangam poets; trade with Rome and West Asia in spices, timber, ivory, pearls; issued coins with royal emblem"],
  ["Pandyas", "Madurai", "Mentioned by Megasthenes; famous for **pearls** (Kharavela imported them); naval power; inscriptions show concern for subjects and tolerance of all beliefs"]]),
 ("note", "NOTE", "**Silappadikaram** (Tale of the Anklet), composed soon after the Sangam collections: Kannagi's husband Kovalan, living in Puhar, wasted his wealth on a dancer, returned, and went with Kannagi to Madurai. Falsely accused of stealing her anklet, he was executed by the Pandya king. Kannagi proved his innocence with her second anklet; the king died of shock; she cursed Madurai, which burned. She was honoured as a goddess in the Chera land and is still worshipped in Tamil Nadu and Kerala. Theme: **justice and the ruler's dharma**."),
 ("h2", "7.6 Foreign Rulers in the North-West"),
 ("h3", "Indo-Greeks"),
 ("ul", ["Descended from the satraps Alexander left; after the Mauryas they took the north-west (present Pakistan, Afghanistan). Greek and Indian elements blended in governance, art, language.",
  "**Heliodorus pillar** (Vidisha, MP): erected by an Indo-Greek ambassador who praised **Vasudeva** as 'god of gods' and noted three virtues: self-restraint, charity, consciousness.",
  "Coins (gold, silver, copper, nickel) show a king on one side and Greek deities or Indian ones (Vasudeva-Krishna, Lakshmi) on the other; they are the main source about these rulers."]),
 ("h3", "Shakas (Indo-Scythians)"),
 ("ul", ["Ruled the north-west from the late 2nd century BCE to the 5th century CE, until the Kushanas.",
  "The **Shaka Samvat** calendar developed then: 78 years behind the Gregorian (79 for January-March). Adopted as the **Indian National Calendar in 1957**."]),
 ("h3", "Kushanas"),
 ("ul", ["From Central Asia, entered India about the **2nd century CE**; empire stretched from Central Asia to northern India. **Kanishka** was their greatest king (headless statue, inscription 'Maharaja Rajadhiraja Devaputra Kanishka').",
  "Coins show Kanishka with the **Buddha** (inscribed in Greek script) or **Shiva with Nandi** — reflecting respect for many faiths.",
  "Controlled a large part of the **Silk Route**, connecting India with Asia and the West, boosting trade."]),
 ("table", ["Feature", "Gandhara School", "Mathura School"], [
  ["Region", "Western Punjab / north-west", "Mathura, Uttar Pradesh"],
  ["Material", "Grey-black **schist**", "**Red sandstone**"],
  ["Influence", "Greco-Roman + Indian; realistic anatomy, flowing robes", "Distinctly Indian; fuller figures with smooth modelling"],
  ["Subjects", "Many fine Buddha images, bodhisattvas", "Buddha, Shiva, Lakshmi, Kubera, yakshas and yakshinis (moustache in some)"]]),
 ("ul", ["This period saw deities depicted in human form (e.g., Surya), laying the base for temple art. **Sanskrit literature** flourished and the **Mahabharata and Ramayana** took shape.",
  "Message: Indian heritage grew by **interaction and assimilation**, with Indian (Hindu and Buddhist) themes dominant."]),
 ("table", ["Ruler / dynasty", "Approximate period"], [
  ["Pushyamitra Shunga", "185 BCE onward"], ["Kharavela (Chedi)", "1st century BCE"], ["Satavahanas (Gautamiputra Satakarni)", "2nd century BCE to 3rd century CE"],
  ["Karikala (Chola)", "Sangam Age"], ["Indo-Greeks", "After the Mauryas"], ["Shakas", "Late 2nd century BCE to 5th century CE"], ["Kushanas (Kanishka)", "From 2nd century CE"]]),
 ("h2", "7.7 Quick Revision"),
 ("qa", [("Why is the post-Maurya period called the Age of Reorganisation?", "The empire broke into many competing kingdoms and invasions from the north-west reshaped the political map."),
  ("Who founded the Shunga dynasty?", "Pushyamitra Shunga, who assassinated the last Mauryan about 185 BCE."),
  ("What is the ashvamedha yajna?", "A Vedic ritual where a free-roaming horse marks the king's claim over the lands it crosses; if stopped, a battle decides supremacy."),
  ("What does the Bharhut stupa show?", "Shunga-period carved railings with scenes of the Buddha's life — early Buddhist art."),
  ("Where did the Satavahanas rule? Name their capitals.", "The Deccan (Andhra, Telangana, Maharashtra); Amaravati and Pratishthana (Paithan)."),
  ("Why did Satavahana kings use mothers' names?", "It shows the high status of women such as Gautami Balashri; e.g. Gautamiputra Satakarni."),
  ("What is the Hathigumpha inscription?", "Brahmi record in Udayagiri caves of Kharavela's victories and welfare works."),
  ("What is Sangam literature?", "The oldest literature of south India: anthologies by poets' assemblies on love, heroism and society."),
  ("Why is Karikala remembered?", "He defeated the Chera-Pandya alliance and built the Kallanai (Grand Anicut) on the Kaveri."),
  ("Summarise Silappadikaram.", "Kannagi's husband Kovalan is wrongly executed in Madurai; she proves his innocence, curses the city and is honoured as a goddess; it centres on justice."),
  ("Who were the Indo-Greeks? What does the Heliodorus pillar show?", "Rulers descended from Alexander's satraps; the pillar shows a Greek ambassador devoted to Vasudeva — cultural blending."),
  ("What is Shaka Samvat?", "A calendar 78 years behind the Gregorian, adopted as India's National Calendar in 1957."),
  ("Compare Gandhara and Mathura art.", "Gandhara: schist, Greco-Roman influence; Mathura: red sandstone, Indian style.")]),
 ],
 takeaways=["Mauryan break-up (**185 BCE**) → many kingdoms + foreign invasions = **Age of Reorganisation**.",
  "**Shungas** (Bharhut, ashvamedha, Patanjali), **Satavahanas** (Deccan, trade with Rome), **Chedis** (Kharavela).",
  "South: **Cholas, Cheras, Pandyas**, **Sangam literature**, Silappadikaram, Kallanai.",
  "**Indo-Greeks, Shakas, Kushanas** entered via the north-west and absorbed Indian culture.",
  "**Gandhara and Mathura** art; **Shaka Samvat** is India's national calendar.",
  "Trade (Silk Route, Roman trade) and cultural fusion marked the age."])
